From: Soe Thura <6492082+thixpin@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 13:19:59 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] XEX BASIC compression: allocate the declared image size

Xex2LoadImage reports image.size = security->imageSize, but for BASIC
compression it allocated only the sum of the expanded blocks. When the blocks
expand to less than the declared size (Forza Motorsport 4: 0xF48000 of
0xF50000 bytes), every access to the declared tail read past the allocation.

Allocate the declared size, zero-filled, so the reported extent is always
backed. Reject the image (an empty result) when the blocks expand beyond the
declared size or their data runs past the end of the file. Block sizes are
summed in 64 bits so 32-bit fields cannot wrap past these checks, and a
format header too short to hold any block is rejected.
---
 XenonUtils/xex.cpp | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/XenonUtils/xex.cpp b/XenonUtils/xex.cpp
index 4e79042..21ee0ef 100644
--- a/XenonUtils/xex.cpp
+++ b/XenonUtils/xex.cpp
@@ -172,15 +172,25 @@ Image Xex2LoadImage(const uint8_t* data, size_t dataSize)
         else if (fileFormatInfo->compressionType == XEX_COMPRESSION_BASIC)
         {
             auto* blocks = reinterpret_cast<const Xex2FileBasicCompressionBlock*>(fileFormatInfo + 1);
+            if (fileFormatInfo->infoSize < sizeof(Xex2FileBasicCompressionInfo))
+                return {};
             const size_t numBlocks = (fileFormatInfo->infoSize / sizeof(Xex2FileBasicCompressionInfo)) - 1;
 
-            imageSize = 0;
+            // Sizes are summed in 64 bits: 32-bit block fields must not wrap past the checks below.
+            uint64_t expandedSize = 0, sourceSize = 0;
             for (size_t i = 0; i < numBlocks; i++)
             {
-                imageSize += blocks[i].dataSize + blocks[i].zeroSize;
+                expandedSize += uint64_t(blocks[i].dataSize) + blocks[i].zeroSize;
+                sourceSize += blocks[i].dataSize;
             }
 
-            result = std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>(imageSize);
+            // The image is reported with the security header's size (image.size below), which
+            // can exceed the expanded blocks: allocate that whole extent, zero-filled. Reject
+            // blocks that expand beyond it, or whose data runs past the end of the file.
+            if (expandedSize > security->imageSize.get() || sourceSize > dataSize - header->headerSize)
+                return {};
+            imageSize = size_t(expandedSize);
+            result = std::make_unique<uint8_t[]>(security->imageSize.get());
             auto* destData = result.get();
 
             for (size_t i = 0; i < numBlocks; i++)
